@meta
id: 06-07
title: Going back with F
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: find-f
par: 32
minutes: 4
vscode: End, then click back between the comma and the handler
open: routes.ts

@brief
The refund route is missing its statement terminator, and it is missing the
body validation middleware. Both need fixing:

    const refundRoute = router.post('/orders/:id/refunds', requireAuth, validateBody, refundHandler);

`validateBody` goes between `requireAuth` and `refundHandler`, so it belongs
just after the last comma on the line. You will be at the far right of the
line by the time you get to it, which means travelling leftwards to a
character rather than rightwards.

@keys
| `f` | find forward on this line |
| `F` | find backward on this line |
| `a` | insert to the right of the cursor |

@hints
Fix the terminator first, with the key that appends at the end of the line.
That leaves the cursor at the right-hand end.
--
`F` is `f` pointing the other way: `F,` jumps back onto the nearest comma to
the left of the cursor.
--
`A` `;` `Esc`, then `F,`, then `a`, then type ` validateBody,` — note the
leading space — then `Esc`.

@solution
    A ; <Esc> F , a  validateBody, <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`f` and `F` are the same instrument aimed in opposite directions, and the
shifted key meaning backwards is a pattern you will meet again and again in
vim. Anything you can say forwards you can say backwards, and getting into
the habit of thinking which way is the character rather than reaching for
the mouse is most of what makes editing feel quick.

Landing on the comma rather than before `refundHandler` is deliberate. The
comma is a single, unmissable character; the identifier is a word you would
have to aim at more carefully. Aim for the punctuation, then use `a` or `i`
to pick which side of it you end up on.

@start routes.ts
const refundRoute = router.post('/orders/:id/refunds', requireAuth, refundHandler)

@expected routes.ts
const refundRoute = router.post('/orders/:id/refunds', requireAuth, validateBody, refundHandler);
